--TEST--
A string literal at a parameter typed `property<T>` alone is checked while compiling and needs no `as`, at a static method, an instance method, a `?property<T>` and a `static` parameter, and a parameter typed `property<T>|string` still takes any string — `rule:types/property-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A string literal at a `property<Order>` parameter is checked when the program compiles.
// You do not need to write `as property<Order>` there.
class Order {
    public string $id = "o1";
    public int $total = 40;

    public static function sortBy(property<static> $field): string {
        return "sorted by " . $field;
    }
}

class Invoice extends Order {
    public string $due = "soon";
}

class Report {
    public static function column(property<Order> $field): string {
        return "column " . ($field as string);
    }

    public static function maybe(?property<Order> $field): string {
        return $field == null ? "none" : "maybe " . ($field as string);
    }

    public static function label(property<Order>|string $field): string {
        return "label " . ($field as string);
    }

    public function read(Order $order, property<Order> $field): string {
        return $order->$field as string;
    }
}

echo Report::column("total"), "\n";

// A `?property<Order>` parameter also checks the literal.
echo Report::maybe("id"), "\n";
echo Report::maybe(null), "\n";

// With `static`, the names come from the class that the call names.
echo Invoice::sortBy("due"), "\n";

// The literal gives a key, so the method can read the property with it.
Report $report = new Report();
echo $report->read(new Order(), "total"), "\n";

// A parameter typed `property<Order>|string` takes any string.
echo Report::label("anything"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
column total
maybe id
none
sorted by due
40
label anything
